					(size 1 1)
					(thickness 0.15)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Value" "12V 600W TVS"
			(at 0 3.1 0)
			(layer "F.Fab")
			(hide yes)
			(uuid "e12c06e6-56b3-4534-a767-0b786fa4af5b")
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
					(thickness 0.15)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Datasheet" "https://www.bourns.com/docs/product-datasheets/smbj-q.pdf"
			(at 0 0 0)
			(layer "F.Fab")
			(hide yes)
			(uuid "0518aaba-a06e-47b0-ac9e-915bc6d126fe")
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
					(thickness 0.15)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Description" "TVS 12V standoff 600W unidirectional DO-214AA SMB AEC-Q101, VBR 13.3V min, VC 19.9V at 30.2A, IR 1uA max at VRWM, -55 to +150C TJ - 8V8 rail clamp. NOT A ZENER: the D_Zener symbol is only the schematic shape, a zener of the same voltage has orders of magnitude less surge capability and must not be substituted. Cathode (pin 1) to +8V8, anode to GND. THE STANDOFF IS SET BY THE OTP RAIL, NOT THE 8.8V RAIL: fitting R604 lifts the rail to 11.1V for OTP programming, so a 10V part (VBR min 11.1V) would conduct hard the moment R604 goes in. 12V leaves 2.2V to VBR min. Its real job is protecting Q201, not the BQ79616 - LDOIN is regulated by the NPNB loop so it stays at 6V whatever the rail does, but without the clamp Q201 would dissipate (67-6)V x 25mA = 1.5W in SOT-223 and cook; with it, 0.2W. In a mis-regulation fault it sits at about 1.4W, which an SMB cannot hold indefinitely - it will eventually fail short, collapsing the rail and killing the board safely"
			(at 0 0 0)
			(layer "F.Fab")
			(hide yes)
			(uuid "1669ce94-5542-4d28-b388-95518c363ed3")
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1 1)
					(thickness 0.15)
				)
			)
		)
